<p>The United States and Venezuela say they will restore diplomatic and consular relations, ending a break that began in 2019 and reopening formal channels for services such as consular assistance. US officials framed the move as part of a broader push to support stability and a peaceful political transition in Venezuela after US forces captured Nicolás Maduro in January and transferred him to detention in New York, according to multiple reports. Several outlets described the decision as a notable thaw with practical benefits, including the return of consular services for citizens. Coverage also pointed to strategic interests—such as Washington’s push for access to Venezuelan minerals—suggesting the renewed relationship will blend political and economic priorities as Venezuela’s interim authorities try to steer the country forward.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qMQM6rtsO8exQXnbuW2xKdb9Dm_klquXx2rY_7rAuhl3co6sfYalLVa526A10hh9mfkyc6C2P2GJgA-OXxV-NccxClTM0mQeJ_6t4G3Z54SfDIeRxiGsNtPRUOV38VVTwX21VVEj5v94d1iDSyd3PdEfuRBHRcJnGxaYbP9vlBtaGH5Ruqk7szLNkMptfLzsV2qhdxjUfVKqVnkKc6-vdg' alt='' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cabinet-level visit: PBS said the announcement came at the end of a two-day trip to Venezuela by US Secretary of the Interior Doug Burgum.</li><li>Joint stability goal: The BBC reported the US cast the restart as a joint effort to promote stability.</li><li>Minerals focus: The Straits Times and RFI said Washington is also pressing for minerals access as ties resume.</li><li>Interim leadership: Firstpost reported Delcy Rodríguez has been sworn in as interim president after Maduro’s capture.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>US State Department: The State Department announced the United States and Venezuela’s interim authorities had agreed to restore diplomatic and consular ties after a rupture dating to 2019. (<a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/03/06/us-and-venezuela-restore-diplomatic-and-consular-ties-after-seven-year-rupture'>MercoPress</a>)</li><li>US government (stated aim): US officials described the policy as creating conditions for a peaceful transition to a democratically elected government in Venezuela. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/us-venezuela-agree-to-re-establish-diplomatic-and-consular-relations'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>US government (resource interest): Reporting said Washington is pursuing access to Venezuelan minerals alongside the diplomatic reset. (<a href='https://www.rfi.fr/en/international-news/20260306-us-venezuela-restore-ties-as-washington-pushes-for-minerals-access'>RFI</a>)</li><li>Venezuelan interim authorities: Venezuela’s interim authorities participated in the agreement to restore relations, formalizing the post-Maduro shift described across outlets. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/u-s-and-venezuela-agree-to-reestablish-diplomatic-ties-in-major-shift-after-maduros-ouster'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/03/06/us-and-venezuela-restore-diplomatic-and-consular-ties-after-seven-year-rupture'>US and Venezuela restore diplomatic and consular ties after seven-year rupture</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-venezuela-to-restore-diplomatic-ties/a-76238474'>US, Venezuela to restore diplomatic ties</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/us-venezuela-establish-diplomatic-relations-first-time-since-2019/'>U.S., Venezuela to establish diplomatic relations for first time since 2019</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/u-s-and-venezuela-agree-to-reestablish-diplomatic-ties-in-major-shift-after-maduros-ouster'>U.S. and Venezuela agree to reestablish diplomatic ties in major shift after Maduro&#x27;s ouster</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/months-after-maduros-capture-us-and-venezuela-agree-to-re-establish-ties-13986700.html'>Months after Maduro&#x27;s capture, US and 